/*
	The two pieces printed behind the admin console.

	One glyph, one weight — a dot matrix, not a shading ramp: tone would turn
	the forms into grey slabs at this size. Neither is hand-typed; both are
	baked here, so nothing is computed at runtime:

	  · creationOfAdam — traced from the fresco, cropped to the band the two
	    arms occupy: the gesture is the subject, so the rest of the scene is
	    not in the frame. The band clears the top of God's arm, which rises to
	    the right — cropped tight, that arm ends on a flat horizontal cut. Skin is separated from the plaster by warmth (r - b,
	    which the grey wall fails and skin passes), then rendered as a halftone
	    — density carries the tone, so shadow is dense, lit skin is nearly
	    bare, and the silhouette fades out at its edge instead of stopping. A
	    contour term thickens the dots where the modelling turns fastest, the
	    way an engraver leans on a burin, which is what keeps the anatomy
	    legible at this sparseness. Grain is value noise, not an ordered
	    dither: a dither lattice across the flat mid-tones of the forearms
	    makes the whole piece read as stripes. The two halves are then split
	    at the emptiest column of the middle third and spread apart: the login
	    card covers the centre, so the fingertips converging there are the one
	    part nobody sees. Spreading moves each hand out toward its own edge.

	  · worldMap — coastlines as lon/lat polygons on an equirectangular
	    projection, sampled 2x2 per cell so a cell holding any coast keeps
	    its dot. Deliberately coarse; it is a backdrop, not an atlas.

	The generators live in git history.
*/

